feat: v0.0.3 — open UI in a chromeless browser window on launch

Users wanted a real window that opens instead of manually typing a URL
into a browser. Solved with zero new dependencies by launching Edge/Chrome
in --app mode, which strips the URL bar, tabs and menus so the window
feels like a native desktop application.

Changes:
- internal/window: new package exposing Open(url, log); probes msedge,
  chrome, google-chrome, chromium, brave in that order and launches with
  --app=<url> --window-size=1200,800; falls back to the OS default browser
  (start / open / xdg-open) if no chromium-family browser is on PATH
- internal/config: HOUND_HEADLESS env var to skip the window (used by
  Docker/servers where there is no display)
- cmd/hound: waits 300ms for the HTTP server to be reachable then calls
  window.Open unless headless mode is on
- docker-compose.yml: sets HOUND_HEADLESS=1 so containers stay headless
- static/index.html + VERSION: bumped to 0.0.3
- .gitignore: exclude SQLite WAL sidecar files (-shm, -wal)

Later plans (v1.x): migrate to Wails or webview_go for a truly native
window with system tray. For now the --app trick gets us 90 percent of
the desktop-app feel with 5 percent of the complexity.
